## What makes a good belief

- **Durable, not transient.** "The user prefers terse answers" is durable. "The user said hi" is not.
- **Self-contained.** It must make sense on its own, with no reference to "the above" or "this task" -- it will be retrieved in isolation, far from these episodes.
- **One of three kinds:**
  - `fact` -- a stable truth about the user, their domain, or their world ("the user's primary repository is memql").
  - `preference` -- a stated or inferred preference ("the user prefers pull requests over direct commits").
  - `outcome` -- a learned result of trying something ("running the full test suite without `-short` times out; use `-short` for quick checks", "approach Y failed twice for task Z").
- **Specific.** Name the thing. "Tool X reliably solves problem P" beats "some tools work".

## When to refuse

Set `distilled: false` when the cluster is just transient chatter, one-off tool noise, or anything you would not want surfacing in a future unrelated task. Be conservative: every belief you mint costs recall budget and has to be decayed back out later if it turns out to be noise. It is better to keep nothing than to keep a weak belief.

## Confidence

Set `confidence` by how strongly the episodes support the belief:
- Many episodes agreeing, or an explicit user statement: 0.7-0.9.
- A reasonable inference from a few episodes: 0.4-0.6.
- A weak signal you are only marginally willing to keep: 0.2-0.3 (consider refusing instead).
